## 2. Power supply wiring 🔋

3.7 V lithium battery

Protected charging module (TP4056)

USB charging input

ON/OFF switch → Buck converter

Set and measure the required output voltage first

Arduino Nano + sensors + LCD

Important safety notes:

- A buck converter only reduces voltage; it cannot boost a battery voltage that has fallen below the required output. A 3.7 V battery is about 4.2 V when fully charged and decreases during discharge, so check whether your chosen converter can maintain the required output.
- Never connect the battery directly to the Arduino's 5 V pin. Verify the converter output with a multimeter before connecting the board.
- Confirm the dust sensor's required voltage and current. Some sensors need more current than a small battery or converter can reliably provide.
- Do not assume the charging module supports powering the device while charging. Use a suitable power-path circuit if simultaneous operation and charging are required.

